WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.830
A student project stored passwords as

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.810
plain SHA two fifty six hashes.

3
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.680
When the table leaked in a class

4
00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.880
exercise, a laptop recovered half

5
00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:14.400
of them in an afternoon.

6
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.360
Today: what a cryptographic hash

7
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:22.100
guarantees, why passwords need salted slow

8
00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.280
hashes, and where a second factor really

9
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:25.680
helps.

10
00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.830
A cryptographic hash maps any input

11
00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.670
to a short fixed-length digest.

12
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.670
It should be infeasible to find an

13
00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.810
input from a digest, or two inputs

14
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:39.930
with the same digest.

15
00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:42.210
Change one bit, and the

16
00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.770
digest changes completely.

17
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.050
General hashes are designed to be fast.

18
00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.300
A graphics card can try

19
00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.060
billions of guesses a second.

20
00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.250
People choose predictable

21
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.210
passwords, so an attacker hashes a

22
00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.910
dictionary and looks for matches.

23
00:01:00.290 --> 00:01:02.110
Here is the relative rate of

24
00:01:02.110 --> 00:01:04.050
guesses on one graphics card.

25
00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:07.770
A plain fast hash allows vastly more

26
00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.110
guesses than a slow, memory-hard function

27
00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:13.330
tuned to take a tenth of a second per

28
00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:13.630
login.

29
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.510
Store passwords with a unique random

30
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.910
salt per user and a slow function such

31
00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:23.810
as bcrypt, scrypt or Argon two.

32
00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:27.850
The salt stops precomputed tables and

33
00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.090
makes identical passwords look different.

34
00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:33.010
The cost factor slows every guess.

35
00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:36.790
A second factor means a stolen

36
00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:38.590
password alone is not enough.

37
00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.600
One-time codes help against reuse,

38
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.620
but can still be phished.

39
00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.810
Hardware keys bound to the site's

40
00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.810
address resist phishing, because they

41
00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.390
will not sign for a fake domain.

42
00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:53.200
Quick check!

43
00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.910
Two users have the same password.

44
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.880
With a unique salt each, what do

45
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:03.060
their stored hashes look like?

46
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:04.560
Different.

47
00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:07.830
So an attacker cannot spot shared

48
00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:10.270
passwords, and must attack each one

49
00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:10.670
separately.

50
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.710
A precomputed table for one salt

51
00:02:13.710 --> 00:02:15.390
is useless for the next.

52
00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:18.930
In the app, open the hash lab.

53
00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.780
Change one letter of the input and

54
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.340
compare the digests, then add a salt and

55
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.480
watch identical passwords diverge.

56
00:02:28.590 --> 00:02:29.350
Your turn.

57
00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:32.350
A staff member typed a password and a

58
00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.150
one-time code into a fake login page.

59
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:37.740
Which second factor would have

60
00:02:37.740 --> 00:02:38.420
stopped the attacker?

61
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.550
A hardware security key

62
00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.310
bound to the real site.

63
00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:46.830
Remember: fast hashes are wrong for

64
00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:50.650
passwords, salt every user, tune a slow

65
00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:53.610
function, and prefer phishing-resistant

66
00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:54.050
factors.

67
00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:58.110
Try it in your next lab: give teams a

68
00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.750
small table of fake users hashed three

69
00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:00.990
ways.

70
00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.950
They time a dictionary attack on each,

71
00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.370
then explain the difference in their

72
00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:06.610
report.
